---
import ranking from "../data/ranking.json";
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";

const { stats } = ranking;
---

<Base
  title="Method"
  description="How Neem computes the heat a child feels in a Delhi schoolyard: open data, SOLWEIG radiation physics, a calibrated in-browser preview, a child thermoregulation model, and the tests that keep them honest."
  path="/method"
  night
>
  <section class="wrap head page-head">
    <h1 class="display method-title">Method</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Every number on this site comes from a model run that anyone can reproduce from public data
      with one command. Here is what the models are, where the data comes from, and where they fall
      short.
    </p>
  </section>

  <article class="wrap body">
    <section class="block rule">
      <h2 class="title">The data</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          <strong>Schools</strong> come from OpenStreetMap: 1,100 in Delhi, 823 with their grounds
          mapped, 242 identifiable as government schools. <strong>Buildings</strong> come from the
          Overture Maps release of September 2026, read straight from its public bucket on AWS. Only
          three of the release's 512 files overlap Delhi, so we select them from the release's STAC
          catalogue rather than scanning all of it: 1.9 million footprints in two minutes.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Trees</strong> come from Meta and WRI's global canopy height map, version 2, at
          about 1.2 m, also on AWS Open Data. <strong>Ground height</strong> comes from the
          Copernicus 30 m elevation model. Both are read as cloud-optimised GeoTIFFs, a few kilobytes
          per school, never downloaded whole. <strong>Weather</strong> is ERA5 reanalysis through
          Open-Meteo for Monday 27 April 2026, the hottest school day before Delhi's summer
          vacation: 42.2 °C at 2 pm, 14% humidity.
        </p>
        <p>
          Open data has a gap. Only about 0.5% of Delhi's buildings carry a height or a floor count,
          so heights mostly come from a prior based on footprint size. At 11 am to 1 pm in late April
          the sun is 70 to 75 degrees up and building shade in a yard is small, so the ranking is far
          more sensitive to trees than to building heights. It still matters in the early morning.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="block rule">
      <h2 class="title">The physics</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          SOLWEIG, from the UMEP project used in urban climate research, computes the full radiation
          budget of a person standing at each square metre: direct sun with shadows from buildings and
          tree crowns, diffuse sky light weighted by how much sky the spot can see, and longwave heat
          from sun-warmed ground and walls. Its output is mean radiant temperature. Combined with air
          temperature, humidity and wind it gives UTCI, the feels-like temperature behind the heat
          stress bands on every map here. We run UMEP's Rust implementation on the laptop GPU: a
          250 m school takes about 4 seconds.
        </p>
        <p>
          The ranking runs every school at 2 m for 9 am, 11 am and 1 pm: {stats.schools} schools in
          about ten minutes. Heat twins run at 1 m for every hour from 7 am to 6 pm.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="block rule">
      <h2 class="title">Instant, then verified</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          SOLWEIG is too slow to follow a mouse. So the planner has two tiers. The preview recasts
          shadows for the edited yard in your browser and predicts the change from the physics run,
          not the absolute value: a per-hour linear model of how mean radiant temperature responds to
          losing direct sun, gaining a crown overhead, and having canopy nearby, fitted for each
          school against a second SOLWEIG run with trees scattered over the yard. Squares a design
          doesn't touch keep their physics value exactly. Where you plant, the preview can be off by
          several degrees at midday, and the page says by how much.
        </p>
        <p>
          "Verify with physics" sends the design to a worker that runs SOLWEIG on the edited school
          and recomputes the child's day, about 10 seconds. The API and worker run in containers
          against an emulated AWS stack locally; the same images are built to run on AWS Lambda.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="block rule">
      <h2 class="title">The child</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          JOS-3 models the body as 17 segments, each with core, muscle, fat and skin, and simulates
          sweating, blood flow and shivering minute by minute. We scale it to a nine-year-old (1.32 m,
          28 kg) and walk it through the Delhi government school morning shift: assembly outdoors at
          7:30, class, recess at 10, class, and the walk home at 12:30. Outdoors it stands in the
          yard's median mean radiant temperature for that hour; classrooms are taken as slightly
          cooler than outdoor air, out of the sun.
        </p>
        <p>
          JOS-3 is validated on adults, and its set points do not scale down: a child-sized body rests
          at about 38 °C core in the model. So we report the rise above the child's own resting
          state, which is what heat-strain studies compare, and water lost to sweat and breath. The
          model does not capture children's lower sweat rates. Read these as indicative.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="block rule">
      <h2 class="title">How we know it works</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Property tests check geometry that must always hold: a taller building never lights a cell
          that was in shade, and planting never adds sun anywhere. Differential tests compare
          independent implementations: our shadow caster agrees with SOLWEIG's own shadows on more
          than 97% of cells, the preview stays within 1 °C of SOLWEIG over a yard it has never seen,
          and the browser's TypeScript port reproduces the Python on a shared fixture. One test exists
          because SOLWEIG quietly rewrites the arrays it is given; the engine now passes it copies.
          The job queue is contract-tested against emulated DynamoDB and SQS, including a redelivered
          message running exactly once.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="block rule">
      <h2 class="title">Built on</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          SOLWEIG and UMEP (GPL-3.0), pythermalcomfort and JOS-3 (MIT), Overture Maps (ODbL), Meta and
          WRI canopy height (CC BY 4.0), Copernicus DEM, OpenStreetMap (ODbL), ERA5 via Open-Meteo,
          numba, DuckDB, rasterio, GeoPandas, FastAPI, Astro and React. Neem itself is GPL-3.0.{" "}
          <a href="https://github.com/riteshroshann/neem">The source is on GitHub.</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .head {
    padding-bottom: var(--s-8);
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--s-6);
  }

  .method-title {
    max-width: 8ch;
  }

  .body {
    padding-bottom: var(--section);
  }

  .block {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 340px) minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: var(--s-8);
    padding-block: var(--s-7) var(--s-8);
  }

  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .block {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      gap: var(--s-5);
    }
  }
</style>
